The basic idea behind this implementation is to use two stacks: one for the items that need to be processed (stack1), and another one (stack2) to store items that have been processed but still need to be added to the front of stack1.

When an item is added to the queue (using the `enqueue` method), it is simply appended to stack1.

When an item needs to be removed from the front of the queue (using the `dequeue` method), we first check if stack1 is empty. If it is, we need to move all items from stack2 to stack1 (in reverse order) before popping the first item from stack1. This ensures that the next item popped from stack1 will be the item at the front of the queue.

The `peek` method works similarly, but it only needs to move items from stack2 to stack1 if stack1 is empty.

The `is_empty` method simply checks if both stacks are empty.

The `__len__` and `__str__` methods are provided for convenience, but are not strictly necessary for the functionality of the queue.

